course: dictionary
lesson: 7487
title: The many meanings of possibility
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one to use. «There is a possibility of rain.» Here possibility means the chance that something might happen, so you know rain could come but is not certain.
explain: «The old shed has possibilities.» Here the word means the good things the shed could become, such as a workshop or a playroom. Read the words around the word to decide: are they talking about a chance, a choice, or what something could become?
explain: In maths, possibilities are the different outcomes you could get. When you roll a dice, each of the six numbers is a possibility. Listing them all helps you work out how likely each result is.
fact: Possibility means something that might happen or might be true.
fact: A possibility can be a chance, a choice, or something good that could become real.
fact: «A strong possibility» means something is quite likely to happen, but it is not certain.
fact: «Rule out the possibility» means decide that something cannot happen.
fact: In maths, a dice has 6 possibilities, because it can land on 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or 6.
exercise: «There is a possibility of rain.» What does possibility mean here? | *The chance that rain could come | A sunny day with no clouds | Rain that has already fallen | A wet coat
exercise: «The old shed has possibilities.» What does possibilities mean here? | Times the shed has been painted | Holes in the roof | *Good things the shed could become | Rooms the shed has
exercise: How many possibilities are there when you roll a dice? | Five | *Six | Twelve | One
test: «A password of 3 words chosen from a list of 1000 has 1000 × 1000 × 1000 = 1,000,000,000 (a thousand million) possibilities.» How many possibilities is that? | *A thousand million | One million | A thousand | Three thousand
test: Mia says «a strong possibility» means it will happen for certain. What does a strong possibility really mean? | It is certain and cannot change | *It is likely to happen, but it is not certain | It is a tiny, unlikely chance | It will never happen
exam: What does «rule out the possibility» mean? | Decide that it is sure to happen | Make a list of all the choices | Hope that it will come true | *Decide that it cannot happen
